platform_credit_ledger.sql

# --- Added 2026-09-18 --------------------------------------------------
# Standing regression coverage for two of the four bug classes found in the
# 2026-09-18 finance-console and notification-deliverability audits (PR #680,
# #681), added so each stays closed by CI rather than only by memory/CLAUDE.md
# prose. private.remap_notification_channel() no longer honours a dead
# whatsapp/sms notification_channel_preference as a direct lock-in (both
# channels are 100% failure live) -- proves sms/whatsapp preferences fall
# through to the same push-or-fallback ladder no-preference already used,
# email is still honoured, and a sabotage run (reinstating the old lock-in)
# reopens the hole. The payment-activation fee-tolerance bug (PR #531) already
# has standing coverage in payment_activation_amount_and_reference.sql above
# (section 8); the false-consent onlyTypes bug (PR #661) is TypeScript-level
# application logic, not DB logic, and is covered instead by
# apps/web/src/app/onboarding/consent-onlytypes-scope.test.ts (Jest).
notification_channel_preference_dead_channel_no_lockin.sql

# private.finance_post_platform_credit_ledger_entry()'s 'spend' branch used to
# credit revenue account 4100 (immediate) unconditionally, with no check for
# whether the purchased service has a bounded access window -- the equivalent
# card-payment path correctly defers to 2000 + a revenue_recognition_schedules
# row when service_products.access_duration_days is set. Proves a bounded-
# access spend defers to 2000 with a matching recognition schedule, a
# genuinely unbounded (one-off) product still posts straight to 4100 with no
# schedule created, and a sabotage run (reverting to unconditional 4100)
# reopens the hole on the bounded case.
platform_credit_spend_revenue_deferral.sql

# private.finance_reverse_entry read finance_journal_entries with a plain
# SELECT and only later set is_reversed = true, so two concurrent reversal
# attempts on the same entry (finance_reverse_journal's synchronous path, or
# finance_approve_request's journal_reversal branch) could both read
# is_reversed = false before either committed -- a real race, though NOT the
# double-financial-effect one it first looked like: a pre-existing unique
# index on (source, source_ref) already makes a genuine second contra
# posting for the same entry impossible, so what the race actually produced
# was a raw, unhandled duplicate-key error (or a misleading false-success)
# instead of a clean business error. The FOR UPDATE fix is still correct --
# see the migration's own header for the full, corrected account, and this
# proof's SABOTAGE 3 for how it verifies that corrected claim rather than the
# original wrong one. finance_approve_request also called finance_reverse_entry
# with no prior check that the target entry was reversible at all, so a
# second pending request approved after the first already reversed the entry
# (or a request whose target entry no longer exists) raised and rolled back
# the WHOLE approval transaction, leaving that request stuck at
# status='pending' forever -- this half is unaffected by the correction
# above and is a real, severe bug. A .sh proof, not .sql, because the first
# finding specifically needs two real overlapping transactions -- see its
# own header for why a single-session BEGIN/ROLLBACK script can't prove a
# lock actually blocks a second session. Proves exactly one reversal
# succeeds under concurrent direct calls (check 1) and under concurrent
# approvals of two duplicate pending requests -- the loser cleanly rejected,
# never stuck pending (check 2) -- and two sabotage runs (reverting the FOR
# UPDATE lock; reverting only the approve-time pre-check) each reopen their
# respective hole. Also fixed in the same migration+PR: the CHECK constraint
# that had silently rejected every journal_reversal request since
# 20260917235414 shipped (drop constraint if exists + a closing DO
# assertion), and the Approvals UI/Zod schemas, which would have thrown on
# the whole approvals query the first time a real journal_reversal row
# existed (apps/web/src/lib/finance/schemas.ts only accepted
# request_type in manual_journal/period_lock).
finance_reversal_concurrent_lock.sh

# Two successive /code-review high passes on migration 20260918100746
# (adding clinical_encounter_notes.clinical_encounter_id), both before it
# ever reached a PR, found 4 real bugs: (1) the sync trigger's row()
# construct had an untyped string literal that raised "cannot compare
# dissimilar column types text and unknown" on every real insert/update with
# a link column set; (2) two AFTER UPDATE triggers on async_consults fire in
# undocumented alphabetical order, so an auto-drafted note could be created
# before its clinical_encounters row existed and never get linked; (3)
# clinical_encounter_id could be set directly by any clinical-tier staff
# member via a plain UPDATE despite its own "never client-supplied" comment
# -- fixed in 20260918101643; (4) the fix for (2) itself introduced a new
# regression, caught by the SECOND review pass on that fix: its fix-up
# UPDATE could abort an entirely unrelated, session-less source-table write
# (e.g. a Zoom webhook completing a video consultation) by tripping
# clinical_encounter_notes_enforce_attribution's UPDATE branch, which has no
# trusted-system escape hatch -- fixed in 20260918103547 by making the
# fix-up best-effort (exception-wrapped, never blocks the write it
# piggybacks on). Proves all four fixes end-to-end via real
# async-consult-answered and video-consultation-completed flows, with a
# sabotage control for bug (1).
clinical_encounter_note_link_bugs.sql

# 20260922183343 added an explicit ::care_access_category cast to 16 RLS
# policies whose can_read_clinical(patient_id, '<literal>') call was left
# bare by earlier migrations, closing the ambiguous-overload hole a future
# CREATE POLICY re-resolving that same bare text would hit now that
# can_read_clinical has 3 live overloads (a real incident on a separate,
# unmerged admin/support "view as" branch, which is where this was found).
# Proves all 16 policies carry the cast, and sabotages by creating a scratch
# policy with the pre-fix bare text against a real table to confirm it still
# fails to even CREATE (42725) today.
can_read_clinical_bare_literal_policy_cast.sql

